the wildfires are spread across both  ends of California campfire to the north  and the Wolsey and Hill fires near Los  Angeles what's made them so deadly is  the speed at which they traveled camp  fire grew by 10,000 acres in less than  two hours at its peak that's more than  one football pitch a second  they've been fueled by high winds lower  than normal humidity and extremely dry  conditions California is in its sixth  year of drought temperatures are now one  to two degrees higher because of global  warming this summer was one of the  hottest on record and has been very  little rain since May on top of this the  seasonal Santa Ana winds have brought  dry desert air and gusts of up to 70  miles an hour from Southern California's  mountain rages towards the coast  President Trump has blamed the state's  poor forest management for the fires but  the federal government is actually the  largest owner of forest lands  responsible for 57 percent of  California's 33 million acres of tree  cover fire officials have also  highlighted that the blazes in the South  aren't forest fires but urban interface  fires as people increasingly move into  areas near forests and brush land known  as the urban wildlife interface there's  a higher risk of wildfires being  accidentally or intentionally ignited it  also makes them harder to fight the US  Forestry Service has successfully  controlled fires for many years but  that's an our vegetation to build up  it's now begun a program of controlled  burns to remove that dry vegetation and  restrict the spread of wildfires but it  has a huge area to cover
 

